IMPORT prelude

-- Central Provident Fund Act 1953 (Singapore) -- contributions by self-employed persons: the duty (s 9A), what counts as
-- "income" (s 9A(3)), the crediting of those contributions (s 13(4)(b), s 13A), interest on arrears (s 9(2), (3)), the
-- Minister's waiver (s 9C), the offence and penalty for not paying (s 58(1)(e), s 61(1), s 61B(1), s 63(1)), and the point at
-- which platform remuneration leaves s 9A for s 8A and the Fourth Schedule (s 8A(9); Fourth Schedule paras 4 and 5).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation (version in force from 1 July 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/CPFA1953.txt. s 9A as inserted by Act 37 of 2019; s 8A(9) as inserted by Act 30 of 2024 wef
-- 15 December 2024.
--
-- WHY. Added on 11 October 2026 for REQ-0268 (Jun Hao scenario, event j043): "A self-employed delivery rider declares $31,000 of
-- trade income for a year. What Medisave contribution must he make, and by when?" He was born on 30 August 2006 and is 21 when he
-- files in April 2028.
--
-- WHAT THE ACT DOES NOT SAY. The Act imposes the duty but leaves every figure to regulations made under s 77(1):
--   * who is a "self-employed person" (s 2: "has the meaning given by any regulations made under section 77(1)(e)");
--   * how the income is determined and what income is excluded (s 9A(1), (3));
--   * the amount ("computed in the manner prescribed", s 9A(1)(a)), which may differ by class of income or person (s 9A(2));
--   * the time ("before the expiry of the prescribed period, or any longer period that the Board specifies", s 9A(1)(b));
--   * the interest rate and its start date (s 9(2));
--   * the date from which platform remuneration leaves s 9A (s 8A(9)).
-- None of those regulations is deposited, and the Act does not name them. So the rate, the income floor, the cap, the period and
-- the interest rate are INPUTS here. The Act does not even say "medisave" in s 9A: the contributions are credited "in such manner
-- as the Minister may direct" (s 13(4)(b)), and only s 13A(1)(a) names the medisave account, as one option for a licensing
-- condition.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: estimated contributions by collectors (s 9B) beyond a mention; the information duties (s 9D); the inspector's
-- powers (s 5(4B)); the Fourth Schedule's rates for platform workers (Parts 3 and 4); voluntary contributions (s 13B); the
-- listing of every Act in the Third Schedule.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Central Provident Fund Act 1953 -- self-employed persons`

§§ `9A(1), 8A(9) -- who must pay`

-- s 9A(1): "A self-employed person must pay contributions to the Fund, in respect of the self-employed person's income determined
-- in the manner prescribed by regulations made under section 77(1) -- (a) of an amount computed in the manner prescribed by
-- regulations made under section 77(1); (b) before the expiry of the prescribed period, or any longer period that the Board
-- specifies in any particular case; and (c) in the approved manner."
-- s 2: "'self-employed person' has the meaning given by any regulations made under section 77(1)(e)". That status is an input.
--
-- s 8A(9): "Sections 9A and 9B do not apply to any platform remuneration paid or payable to a platform worker on or after a date
-- prescribed by regulations made under section 77(1)." The date is not in the Act; whether it has passed is an input.
-- INFERENCE (labelled): the carve-out is by kind of income, not by person. A platform worker who also has other trade income
-- remains bound by s 9A on that other income.

-- s 9A(3): "'income' means assessable income from any trade, business, profession or vocation (other than from employment under a
-- contract of service) ascertained in accordance with section 37(1) of the Income Tax Act 1947, and excludes any income prescribed
-- by regulations made under section 77(1)."
GIVEN `from a trade, business, profession or vocation` IS A BOOLEAN, `from employment under a contract of service` IS A BOOLEAN, `excluded by the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the receipt counts as income under section 9A(3)` IF
        `from a trade, business, profession or vocation`
    AND (NOT `from employment under a contract of service`)
    AND (NOT `excluded by the regulations`)

GIVEN `is a self-employed person under the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN, `the income is platform remuneration` IS A BOOLEAN, `paid on or after the section 8A(9) prescribed date` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 9A binds this income` IF
        `is a self-employed person under the regulations`
    AND (NOT (`the income is platform remuneration` AND `paid on or after the section 8A(9) prescribed date`))

§§ `Fourth Schedule paras 4, 5 -- which platform workers are Group A`

-- Para 4(1): "A platform worker who was born before 1 January 1995 may opt to be a Group A worker." (3): the option "cannot be
-- withdrawn". Para 5: Part 3 applies to a platform worker "(a) who was born on or after 1 January 1995; or (b) who was born before
-- 1 January 1995 and opts to be a Group A worker". Para 8: Part 4 (Group B) applies, "subject to paragraphs 5, 6 and 7", to a
-- platform worker born before 1 January 2026 [S 886/2025]. Read with para 5, every platform worker born from 1995 is Group A.
-- For a Group A worker the platform operator pays contributions under s 8A(1) and recovers part from the remuneration (s 8A(2)).
GIVEN `year of birth` IS A NUMBER, `opted under paragraph 4(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the platform worker is a Group A worker` IF
        `year of birth` AT LEAST 1995
    OR  `opted under paragraph 4(1)`

§§ `9A(1)(a), (2) -- the amount`

-- The Act prescribes no rate, floor or cap. INFERENCE (labelled): the regulations' scheme is taken to be a rate on income above a
-- floor, capped. All three figures are inputs; s 9A(2) lets them differ by class of income or of person (e.g. by age). The
-- regulations' real table may phase the rate in between bands; this shape does not model that and is right only where the income
-- is in a band with a single rate.
GIVEN income IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed rate` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed income floor` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed cap` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the section 9A contribution` income `the prescribed rate` `the prescribed income floor` `the prescribed cap` MEANS
    IF income AT MOST `the prescribed income floor` THEN 0
    ELSE IF income TIMES `the prescribed rate` GREATER THAN `the prescribed cap` THEN `the prescribed cap`
         ELSE income TIMES `the prescribed rate`

-- s 9C: "The Minister may waive the payment of the whole or any part of any contribution required under section 9A or 9B in any
-- particular case."
GIVEN contribution IS A NUMBER, `waived by the Minister` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the contribution payable after a section 9C waiver` contribution `waived by the Minister` MEANS
    IF `waived by the Minister` AT LEAST contribution THEN 0 ELSE contribution MINUS `waived by the Minister`

§§ `9A(1)(b) -- by when`

-- "before the expiry of the prescribed period, or any longer period that the Board specifies in any particular case". The
-- prescribed period, and the event it runs from, are in the regulations; both are inputs. The Board may only lengthen it.
GIVEN `the prescribed period in days` IS A NUMBER, `the Board's longer period in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the period for paying in days` `the prescribed period in days` `the Board's longer period in days` MEANS
    IF `the Board's longer period in days` GREATER THAN `the prescribed period in days`
    THEN `the Board's longer period in days`
    ELSE `the prescribed period in days`

GIVEN `days since the period began` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed period in days` IS A NUMBER, `the Board's longer period in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 9A contribution is overdue` IF
    `days since the period began` GREATER THAN `the period for paying in days` `the prescribed period in days` `the Board's longer period in days`

§§ `9(2), (3) -- interest`

-- s 9(2): "Where the amount of the contributions a self-employed person is liable to pay under section 9A is not paid within the
-- time prescribed for payment, that person is liable to pay interest on that amount for every day the amount remains unpaid at the
-- rate and commencing from the date prescribed in regulations made under section 77(1)." The rate is an input, taken as a daily
-- rate on the unpaid amount (INFERENCE: simple, not compounded).
-- s 9(3): "The Board may, in any case in which it thinks fit, waive the payment of the whole or part of ... any interest".
GIVEN `the unpaid amount` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed daily rate` IS A NUMBER, `days unpaid from the prescribed date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the interest under section 9(2)` `the unpaid amount` `the prescribed daily rate` `days unpaid from the prescribed date` MEANS
    IF `days unpaid from the prescribed date` AT MOST 0 THEN 0
    ELSE `the unpaid amount` TIMES `the prescribed daily rate` TIMES `days unpaid from the prescribed date`

GIVEN interest IS A NUMBER, `waived by the Board` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the interest payable after a section 9(3) waiver` interest `waived by the Board` MEANS
    IF `waived by the Board` AT LEAST interest THEN 0 ELSE interest MINUS `waived by the Board`

§§ `58(1)(e), 61, 61B, 63 -- not paying`

-- s 58(1): "If any person -- ... (e) fails to pay to the Board within such period as may be prescribed any amount which the person
-- is liable to pay under the provisions of this Act, the person shall be guilty of an offence." s 5(7) confirms the route: a
-- "relevant offence" includes "(d) an offence under section 58(1)(d) or (e) committed by a self-employed person". s 58(1)(b) (the
-- employee / platform-worker limb) does not apply to him.
-- INFERENCE (labelled): the s 58(1)(e) period is taken to be the same period as s 9A(1)(b), lengthened by the Board.
GIVEN `section 9A binds the income` IS A BOOLEAN, `overdue` IS A BOOLEAN, `waived in full under section 9C` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offence under section 58(1)(e) is committed` IF
        `section 9A binds the income`
    AND `overdue`
    AND (NOT `waived in full under section 9C`)

-- s 61(1): no specific penalty is provided for s 58(1)(e), so "(a) a fine not exceeding $5,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not
-- exceeding 6 months or ... both; and (b) if that person is a repeat offender in relation to the same offence, ... a fine not
-- exceeding $10,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding 12 months or ... both." s 61(2)'s minimum fines attach to
-- s 7(5), 8A(5) and 58(1)(b) only, so there is NO minimum fine for a self-employed person's non-payment.
GIVEN `repeat offender` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine under section 61(1) in dollars` `repeat offender` MEANS
    IF `repeat offender` THEN 10000 ELSE 5000

GIVEN `repeat offender` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment under section 61(1) in months` `repeat offender` MEANS
    IF `repeat offender` THEN 12 ELSE 6

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the minimum fine for a self-employed person's non-payment in dollars` MEANS 0

-- s 61B(1): "The court before which any conviction under section 7(3), 8A(3) or 61 is had may in addition to the penalty ...
-- order the person convicted to pay the amount of any contributions together with any interest due thereon". s 65 also lets the
-- Board sue for "Any sum due to the Fund" as a debt due to the Government.
GIVEN contribution IS A NUMBER, interest IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most the court may order paid under section 61B(1)` contribution interest MEANS contribution PLUS interest

-- s 63(1): the Board may compound an offence "prescribed as a compoundable offence" for "a sum not exceeding $1,000". Whether
-- s 58(1)(e) is prescribed is in the Board's rules (s 63(2)); input.
GIVEN `prescribed as compoundable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most the Board may collect to compound in dollars` `prescribed as compoundable` MEANS
    IF `prescribed as compoundable` THEN 1000 ELSE 0

§§ `13(4)(b), 13A -- where the money goes`

-- s 13(4): "The Board must credit to the accounts of a member ..., in such manner as the Minister may direct, every contribution
-- paid into the Fund for the member under -- ... (b) section 9A". The Act does not itself send s 9A money to the medisave account.
-- s 13A(1): a power to license or register under an Act in the Third Schedule "includes a power to impose conditions relating to
-- the payment of contributions into the Fund, pursuant to section 9A, such contributions to be credited -- (a) to the medisave
-- account of the person ...; or (b) as otherwise provided under section 13(4)." The Road Traffic Act 1961 is item 34.
GIVEN `paid as a condition imposed under section 13A(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
`where a section 9A contribution is credited` `paid as a condition imposed under section 13A(1)(a)` MEANS
    IF `paid as a condition imposed under section 13A(1)(a)`
    THEN "the medisave account"
    ELSE "the accounts the Minister directs under section 13(4)"

GIVEN `the licensing Act is in the Third Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the licensing authority may make section 9A contributions a licence condition` IF
    `the licensing Act is in the Third Schedule`
